-- Q1: medium / L2 — Debt vs equity: thin capitalization as strongest indicator
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Whitfield lends $2,000,000 to her wholly owned C corporation, which has total equity of $50,000. The loan has no fixed maturity date, and repayments are made only when the corporation has sufficient earnings. The IRS audits the transaction. What is the most likely outcome?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The loan is recharacterized as an equity contribution, and interest payments are treated as constructive dividends", "The loan remains valid debt because shareholder loans are always respected regardless of capitalization", "The IRS will allow the interest deduction but disallow the principal repayments", "The corporation must repay the entire loan immediately to avoid reclassification"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRS debt-vs.-equity analysis, courts consider multiple factors including the debt-to-equity ratio, fixed maturity, and unconditional repayment obligation. A 40:1 debt-to-equity ratio ($2M / $50K) constitutes extreme thin capitalization, and contingent repayment based on earnings strongly indicates equity, not debt. The loan is reclassified and interest payments become nondeductible constructive dividends. Wrong (B): Shareholder loans are not automatically respected; substance over form applies. Wrong (C): The IRS does not split treatment between interest and principal in reclassification—the entire instrument is recharacterized. Wrong (D): Immediate repayment is not a remedy; the issue is the character of the instrument.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Section 302(b)(3) complete termination with family attribution waiver
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Henderson owns 40% of Apex Corp and her son owns 60%. Apex redeems all of Henderson's shares for $300,000 (basis $80,000). Henderson files a written agreement to notify the IRS of any reacquisition within 10 years, retains no interest in the corporation (including as officer, director, or employee), and has not acquired any stock from a related person within the prior 10 years. How is the redemption treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Dividend treatment because family attribution cannot be waived under Section 302", "Sale/exchange treatment under Section 302(b)(3) with $220,000 capital gain", "Sale/exchange treatment but only if Henderson also waives entity attribution", "Dividend treatment because Henderson constructively owns her son's 60%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 302(b)(3), a complete termination of the shareholder's interest qualifies for sale/exchange treatment. Although Section 318 family attribution normally attributes the son's 60% to Henderson, Section 302(c)(2) permits waiver of family attribution when the shareholder files a written agreement, retains no interest for 10 years, and has no prohibited acquisitions. Henderson meets all conditions, so gain is $300,000 minus $80,000 = $220,000 capital gain. Wrong (A): Family attribution can be waived under 302(c)(2) for complete terminations. Wrong (C): Entity attribution waiver is not required when family attribution waiver satisfies the complete termination test. Wrong (D): This would be correct without the waiver, but Henderson meets all 302(c)(2) requirements.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Constructive dividend: personal use of corporate auto
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Drayton Corp, a C corporation, owns a luxury vehicle with an annual lease value of $15,000. Marcus, the sole shareholder, uses the vehicle 80% for personal purposes and 20% for corporate business. Drayton deducts the entire $15,000 lease cost. How should the IRS treat the personal-use portion?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The personal use is a tax-free fringe benefit for the sole shareholder", "The full $15,000 deduction is disallowed because any personal use taints the entire expense", "$12,000 is a constructive dividend to Marcus and is not deductible by the corporation", "Marcus must reimburse the corporation $12,000 or lose his shareholder status"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): When a C corporation provides personal use of corporate property to a shareholder, the FMV of that personal use is a constructive dividend. The personal-use portion is 80% x $15,000 = $12,000. This amount is treated as dividend income to Marcus to the extent of corporate E&P, and the corporation loses the deduction for that portion. Wrong (A): Personal use of corporate property is not a tax-free fringe benefit for a controlling shareholder—it is a constructive dividend. Wrong (B): Only the personal-use portion ($12,000) is disallowed, not the entire lease cost. Wrong (D): There is no requirement to reimburse; the consequence is dividend treatment, not loss of shareholder status.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Section 7872 $100,000 gift loan exception
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A father makes a $75,000 interest-free gift loan to his daughter. The daughter's net investment income for the year is $800. Under Section 7872, what is the imputed interest for the year?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$75,000 multiplied by the AFR", "$800, limited to the daughter's net investment income", "$3,750, using a 5% AFR on the full loan amount", "$0, because the daughter's net investment income does not exceed $1,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 7872(d)(1), for gift loans between $10,001 and $100,000, imputed interest is limited to the borrower's net investment income. If the borrower's net investment income is $1,000 or less, it is treated as zero. Since the daughter's NII is $800, the imputed interest is $0. Wrong (A): This would apply if the loan exceeded $100,000 or the de minimis exception did not apply. Wrong (B): The NII limitation treats amounts of $1,000 or less as zero, not as the actual NII amount. Wrong (C): The full AFR calculation only applies when NII exceeds $1,000 or the loan exceeds $100,000.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Partnership current distribution: cash exceeds outside basis
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Palmer has an outside basis of $40,000 in JKL Partnership. The partnership makes a current (non-liquidating) cash distribution of $65,000 to Palmer. No property is distributed. What is the tax consequence to Palmer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Palmer recognizes $25,000 of capital gain and has a $0 remaining basis in the partnership", "Palmer recognizes no gain because current distributions are always tax-free", "Palmer recognizes $25,000 of ordinary income and reduces her basis to $0", "Palmer must return the excess $25,000 to the partnership to avoid gain recognition"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 731(a)(1), a partner recognizes gain on a current distribution only to the extent cash received exceeds the partner's outside basis. Palmer receives $65,000 cash against a $40,000 basis, so she recognizes $25,000 of capital gain. Her remaining outside basis is reduced to $0. Wrong (B): Current distributions are generally tax-free, but gain is triggered when cash exceeds outside basis. Wrong (C): The excess is treated as capital gain from the sale of a partnership interest, not ordinary income. Wrong (D): There is no obligation to return excess distributions; the tax code simply triggers gain recognition.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Definition of Section 318 constructive ownership
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$What is the purpose of the constructive ownership rules under Section 318?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To determine the fair market value of corporate stock for gift tax purposes", "To attribute stock ownership among related parties for determining control and redemption treatment", "To calculate the basis of stock received in a Section 351 exchange", "To establish the holding period for capital gains treatment on stock sales"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 318 attributes stock ownership among family members, entities, and option holders to determine actual control for purposes such as related party status under Section 267 and redemption treatment under Section 302. These constructive ownership rules prevent taxpayers from circumventing related-party rules through indirect ownership. Wrong (A): Section 318 does not address gift tax valuations; that is governed by Section 2512. Wrong (C): Stock basis in a Section 351 exchange is determined under Section 358, not Section 318. Wrong (D): Holding periods are governed by Section 1223, not the constructive ownership rules.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Section 318 entity-to-owner proportional attribution
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Carlisle owns 70% of Delta Corp. Delta Corp owns 200 shares of Epsilon Corp (out of 1,000 total shares). Under Section 318 entity-to-owner attribution, how many shares of Epsilon Corp is Carlisle deemed to own constructively?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["200 shares, because all entity-owned stock is attributed in full to any shareholder", "70 shares, because only family attribution applies to corporate stock", "140 shares, attributed proportionally based on Carlisle's 70% ownership of Delta Corp", "0 shares, because entity-to-owner attribution only applies to partnerships"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 318(a)(2)(C), stock owned by a corporation is attributed proportionally to any shareholder who owns 50% or more of the corporation's stock. Carlisle owns 70% of Delta, so she is deemed to own 70% x 200 = 140 shares of Epsilon Corp. Wrong (A): Entity-to-owner attribution is proportional, not full attribution, for corporations. Wrong (B): Entity attribution applies to corporations with 50%+ shareholders; it is not limited to family attribution. Wrong (D): Entity-to-owner attribution applies to both partnerships and corporations under Section 318(a)(2).$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Constructive dividend: shareholder expenses paid by corporation
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Grayson is the sole shareholder of Falcon Corp, a C corporation with $500,000 of accumulated E&P. Falcon Corp pays $35,000 for Grayson's personal vacation and $18,000 for his children's private school tuition. Neither expense has a business purpose. How are these payments treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both payments are deductible by Falcon Corp as ordinary business expenses", "The vacation is a constructive dividend, but tuition payments are excludable fringe benefits", "Both payments are nontaxable gifts from the corporation to Grayson", "$53,000 total is treated as a constructive dividend to Grayson, nondeductible by Falcon Corp"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): When a C corporation pays personal expenses of a shareholder without a business purpose, the payments are constructive dividends to the extent of E&P. The $35,000 vacation plus $18,000 tuition totals $53,000 of constructive dividend income to Grayson. Falcon Corp cannot deduct these amounts because they are distributions, not business expenses. Wrong (A): Personal shareholder expenses with no business purpose are not deductible business expenses. Wrong (B): Tuition payments for a shareholder's children without a business nexus are not excludable fringe benefits. Wrong (C): Corporations cannot make nontaxable gifts to shareholders; such payments are dividends to the extent of E&P.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: hard / L3 — Constructive dividend: bargain sale of corporate property to shareholder
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Garrison Corp, a C corporation with $600,000 of E&P, sells a parcel of land (FMV $250,000, basis $90,000) to its sole shareholder for $100,000. The sale is not at arm's length. What are the tax consequences to Garrison Corp and the shareholder?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Garrison recognizes $10,000 gain; shareholder has $100,000 basis in the land and no dividend income", "Garrison recognizes $160,000 gain and the shareholder has $150,000 constructive dividend plus a $250,000 basis in the land", "Garrison recognizes $10,000 gain; shareholder has $150,000 constructive dividend and a $100,000 basis", "Garrison recognizes no gain because it sold below FMV; shareholder has a $100,000 cost basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 311(b), Garrison Corp recognizes gain as if it sold the property at FMV: $250,000 minus $90,000 = $160,000 gain. The difference between FMV and the price paid by the shareholder ($250,000 minus $100,000 = $150,000) is a constructive dividend to the extent of E&P. The shareholder's basis in the land is FMV ($250,000) because the total consideration is $100,000 cash plus $150,000 constructive dividend. Wrong (A): This ignores both the FMV gain recognition rule and the constructive dividend. Wrong (C): Garrison must recognize gain on the full spread to FMV ($160,000), not just the $10,000 above cost. Wrong (D): Section 311(b) requires gain recognition at FMV regardless of the actual sale price.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Section 302 default dividend treatment: basis transfer
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Fletcher owns 300 shares (basis $60,000) of a C corporation with $200,000 of E&P. The corporation redeems 100 of Fletcher's shares for $50,000. The redemption fails all Section 302(b) tests and is treated as a dividend distribution. What happens to the basis of the redeemed shares?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $20,000 basis of the redeemed shares is lost permanently", "The $20,000 basis of the redeemed shares is added to Fletcher's remaining 200 shares", "Fletcher recognizes a $30,000 capital gain on the redeemed shares", "The basis of all 300 shares is reset to $50,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): When a redemption is treated as a dividend under IRC Section 301 (failing all Section 302(b) tests), the $50,000 payment is a dividend to the extent of E&P. The basis of the redeemed 100 shares ($60,000 x 100/300 = $20,000) is not lost; it is added to the basis of Fletcher's remaining 200 shares, increasing their basis from $40,000 to $60,000. Wrong (A): Basis is never permanently lost in a dividend-treated redemption; it transfers to remaining shares. Wrong (C): Because the redemption fails Section 302(b), it is treated as a dividend, not a sale/exchange—no capital gain calculation applies. Wrong (D): Basis is not reset to the distribution amount; only the redeemed shares' basis is transferred.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — S corp basis ordering: stock reduced before debt
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Chen is the sole shareholder of an S corporation. At the beginning of the year, Chen's stock basis is $20,000 and her debt basis (from a direct loan to the corporation) is $30,000. The S corporation reports a $35,000 ordinary loss for the year. How is Chen's loss deduction and basis affected?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Chen deducts $20,000; only stock basis can absorb losses, not debt basis", "Chen deducts $30,000; debt basis is reduced first before stock basis", "Chen deducts $35,000; stock basis reduced to $0 first, then debt basis reduced from $30,000 to $15,000", "Chen deducts $35,000; stock and debt basis are reduced proportionally"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1366(d)(1), an S corporation shareholder's loss deduction is limited to the sum of stock basis plus debt basis. Losses reduce stock basis first to $0, then reduce debt basis. Chen's $20,000 stock basis absorbs the first $20,000 of loss (stock to $0), and the remaining $15,000 reduces debt basis from $30,000 to $15,000. Total deductible loss is $35,000. Wrong (A): Both stock and debt basis can absorb losses, but stock must be reduced first. Wrong (B): Debt basis is reduced after stock basis reaches $0, not before. Wrong (D): There is no proportional reduction; the ordering is strictly stock first, then debt.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Section 267 controlled group loss disallowance
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Warren owns 100% of both Alpha Corp and Beta Corp. Alpha Corp sells manufacturing equipment (basis $120,000) to Beta Corp for $90,000 (its FMV). What is the tax treatment of the $30,000 loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The loss is fully deductible because both corporations are separate legal entities", "The loss is deductible but must be spread over 5 years using straight-line amortization", "The loss is deductible only to the extent of Alpha Corp's capital gains for the year", "The loss is disallowed under Section 267 because Alpha and Beta are members of the same controlled group"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 267(b)(3), two corporations that are members of the same controlled group are related parties. Since Warren owns 100% of both Alpha and Beta, they are in a controlled group. The $30,000 loss on the sale between them is disallowed under Section 267(a)(1). Beta takes a $90,000 cost basis and may offset future gain by the disallowed loss under Section 267(d). Wrong (A): Separate legal entity status does not override Section 267 related party rules for controlled groups. Wrong (B): There is no amortization provision for disallowed related party losses. Wrong (C): Section 267 completely disallows the loss; it is not limited to capital gains.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Section 7872 compensation-related loan treatment
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Hartwell Corp lends $200,000 to its employee, Blake, at 0% interest. The AFR is 4%. Blake is not a shareholder. Under Section 7872, what is the tax treatment of the $8,000 annual foregone interest?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$8,000 treated as compensation to Blake and interest income to Hartwell Corp", "No tax consequences because Section 7872 only applies to shareholder loans", "$8,000 treated as a gift from Hartwell Corp to Blake", "$8,000 treated as a dividend to Blake"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 7872(c)(1)(B), a below-market loan from an employer to an employee is a compensation-related loan. The foregone interest ($200,000 x 4% = $8,000) is treated as compensation paid to Blake (deductible by the corporation, taxable to Blake as wages) and then recharacterized as interest paid by Blake to the corporation (interest income to Hartwell). Wrong (B): Section 7872 applies to compensation-related loans, corporation-shareholder loans, gift loans, and tax-avoidance loans—not only shareholder loans. Wrong (C): Gift treatment applies to loans between individuals, not employer-employee loans. Wrong (D): Dividend treatment applies to corporation-shareholder loans; Blake is not a shareholder.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Section 302(b)(2) with constructive ownership via Section 318
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A CPA is advising Nguyen, who owns 450 out of 1,000 outstanding shares of Phoenix Corp. Nguyen's wife owns 150 shares, and Nguyen holds options to purchase an additional 100 shares. Phoenix Corp proposes to redeem 300 of Nguyen's directly owned shares. Applying Section 318 constructive ownership rules and the Section 302(b)(2) substantially disproportionate test, what should the CPA advise?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The redemption qualifies as substantially disproportionate because Nguyen's direct ownership drops below 50%", "The redemption fails the substantially disproportionate test because Nguyen's constructive ownership after redemption is 57.14%, which exceeds 50%", "The redemption qualifies because option attribution does not apply to redemption analysis", "The redemption fails because family attribution cannot be combined with option attribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 318, Nguyen constructively owns his wife's 150 shares and the 100 option shares, giving him 450 + 150 + 100 = 700 shares before redemption (70% of 1,000). After redeeming 300 directly owned shares, Nguyen directly owns 150 and constructively owns 250 (wife 150 + options 100) = 400 shares out of 700 outstanding (1,000 minus 300). That is 400/700 = 57.14%, which exceeds 50%. The 302(b)(2) test requires post-redemption ownership below 50%, so it fails. Wrong (A): This ignores constructive ownership; direct ownership alone is insufficient for the analysis. Wrong (C): Option attribution under Section 318(a)(4) always applies in redemption analysis. Wrong (D): Family and option attribution are applied independently and can coexist.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Definition of stock redemption
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 302, what is a stock redemption?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A sale of stock between two unrelated shareholders", "The issuance of new stock to existing shareholders", "A corporation's acquisition of its own stock from a shareholder in exchange for property", "A transfer of stock from a parent corporation to a subsidiary"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 302, a stock redemption occurs when a corporation acquires its own stock from a shareholder in exchange for property (usually cash). The key tax issue is whether the redemption is treated as a sale/exchange (capital gain) or as a distribution (dividend to the extent of E&P). Wrong (A): A sale between unrelated shareholders is a stock sale, not a redemption—a redemption involves the issuing corporation. Wrong (B): Issuing new stock is a stock issuance, the opposite of a redemption. Wrong (D): A parent-to-subsidiary stock transfer is a contribution or reorganization, not a redemption.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Definition of constructive dividend
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$What is a constructive dividend for federal income tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A dividend formally declared and paid by the board of directors", "A dividend paid exclusively from accumulated earnings and profits", "A return of capital distribution that exceeds the shareholder's stock basis", "An economic benefit conferred on a shareholder that is not formally declared as a dividend"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): A constructive dividend occurs when a corporation confers an economic benefit on a shareholder—such as personal expense payments, bargain sales, or rent-free use of property—without formally declaring a dividend. The IRS recharacterizes the benefit as a dividend to the extent of the corporation's E&P. Wrong (A): A formally declared dividend is an actual dividend, not a constructive one. Wrong (B): While dividends come from E&P, this describes actual dividends generally, not the constructive dividend concept. Wrong (C): A return of capital exceeding basis produces capital gain, not a constructive dividend.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Multi-factor debt vs equity analysis
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A CPA evaluates a $1,000,000 advance from sole shareholder Morrison to his C corporation. The corporation has $800,000 in equity. The advance has a written promissory note with a 7-year maturity, bears interest at the AFR, but repayment is subordinated to all other creditors and payments are suspended whenever earnings are insufficient. Morrison's prior $200,000 loan to the corporation (on identical terms) was consistently repaid on schedule. Which characterization should the CPA recommend?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Debt, because the advance bears a reasonable interest rate, has a fixed maturity, and the prior loan history supports debt treatment", "Equity, because subordination and earnings-contingent payments outweigh the formal debt features", "Debt for the first $800,000 and equity for the remaining $200,000 based on a 1:1 debt-to-equity limit", "Equity, because any loan from a sole shareholder is automatically reclassified as equity"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): In debt-vs.-equity analysis, courts weigh multiple factors. The 1.25:1 debt-to-equity ratio ($1M/$800K) is reasonable, the note has a fixed 7-year maturity, interest at AFR is arm's-length, and the borrower's track record of repaying the prior identical loan demonstrates genuine intent to repay. While subordination and earnings-contingent suspension are negative factors, the totality of circumstances favors debt. Wrong (B): Subordination is one negative factor but does not automatically override multiple strong debt indicators including proven repayment history. Wrong (C): There is no statutory 1:1 debt-to-equity limit that triggers proportional reclassification. Wrong (D): Sole-shareholder loans are not automatically reclassified; substance-over-form analysis applies.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Section 311(a): no loss on distribution of depreciated property
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Kessler Corp, a C corporation, distributes equipment with a basis of $80,000 and FMV of $50,000 to its sole shareholder as a dividend. Under Section 311, what gain or loss does Kessler Corp recognize on the distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$30,000 loss, treated the same as a sale at fair market value", "No loss recognized; Section 311(a) disallows loss recognition on distributions of property with FMV below basis", "$30,000 gain, because distributions are always treated as sales at FMV", "No gain or loss because the shareholder takes a carryover basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 311(a), a corporation does not recognize a loss when it distributes property with a FMV less than its adjusted basis. This prevents corporations from generating deductible losses by distributing depreciated property instead of selling it. Section 311(b) requires gain recognition only for appreciated property (FMV exceeds basis). The shareholder takes a basis equal to FMV ($50,000) under Section 301(d). Wrong (A): Section 311(a) specifically disallows loss recognition on property distributions to shareholders. Wrong (C): Gain recognition under Section 311(b) applies only when FMV exceeds basis, not the reverse. Wrong (D): The shareholder takes FMV basis under Section 301(d), not carryover basis; regardless, the corporate-level treatment is no loss.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Partnership current distribution: property basis (carryover limited to remaining basis)
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Porter has an outside basis of $50,000 in RST Partnership. The partnership makes a non-liquidating distribution of $20,000 cash and equipment with an inside basis of $45,000 (FMV $60,000). Porter must determine the basis of the distributed equipment and any gain recognized. What is the correct result?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Equipment basis of $45,000 and $15,000 capital gain recognized", "Equipment basis of $60,000 and no gain recognized", "Equipment basis of $30,000 and no gain recognized", "Equipment basis of $45,000 and no gain recognized"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 732(a), in a current distribution, cash reduces outside basis first: $50,000 minus $20,000 = $30,000 remaining. Property takes a carryover basis but is limited to the partner's remaining outside basis. The equipment's inside basis ($45,000) exceeds Porter's remaining basis ($30,000), so the equipment takes a $30,000 basis. No gain is recognized because cash ($20,000) did not exceed original outside basis ($50,000). Porter's outside basis is reduced to $0. Wrong (A): The carryover basis is capped at remaining outside basis, not the full inside basis, and gain is not triggered when cash does not exceed basis. Wrong (B): FMV is irrelevant for basis determination in partnership distributions; carryover basis rules apply. Wrong (D): The $45,000 carryover basis must be limited to the $30,000 remaining outside basis.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Section 302(b)(4) partial liquidation
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A CPA evaluates Omega Corp's plan to distribute $400,000 to its shareholders in connection with discontinuing its retail division, which had been actively conducted for over 5 years. Omega will continue operating its manufacturing division. The distribution is pro rata to all individual shareholders. The CPA is asked whether the distribution qualifies for sale/exchange treatment. What is the correct analysis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Dividend treatment because pro rata distributions never qualify for sale/exchange treatment", "Sale/exchange treatment because the payout exceeds $100,000", "Dividend treatment because partial liquidations only apply to corporate shareholders", "Sale/exchange treatment under Section 302(b)(4) because the distribution results from a genuine corporate contraction of a qualifying 5-year active trade or business"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 302(b)(4) and Section 302(e), a distribution qualifies as a partial liquidation if it is not essentially equivalent to a dividend at the corporate level and results from the termination of a qualified trade or business actively conducted for at least 5 years. The corporation must continue at least one other active business. Omega meets all requirements. For non-corporate shareholders, this qualifies for sale/exchange treatment even though it is pro rata. Wrong (A): Pro rata distributions can qualify for sale/exchange treatment under the partial liquidation rules for non-corporate shareholders. Wrong (B): Dollar amount is irrelevant to the partial liquidation analysis. Wrong (C): Section 302(b)(4) partial liquidation applies to non-corporate shareholders; it is corporate shareholders who cannot use this provision.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Section 318 family attribution: no siblings
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Which family members are included in Section 318 constructive ownership attribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Spouse, children, grandchildren, and parents", "Spouse, children, parents, siblings, and in-laws", "All family members within three degrees of kinship", "Only spouse and minor children"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 318(a)(1), family attribution includes stock owned by the taxpayer's spouse, children, grandchildren, and parents. Notably, siblings are not included in Section 318 family attribution, which is a common exam distinction. Wrong (B): Section 318 does not include siblings or in-laws in family attribution. Wrong (C): The attribution is limited to specific relationships, not a general kinship degree test. Wrong (D): Adult children, grandchildren, and parents are also included, not just spouse and minor children.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L2 — Constructive dividend: rent-free use of corporate property
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Monroe Corp, a C corporation with ample E&P, allows its sole shareholder to live in a corporate-owned condominium rent-free for the entire year. The fair rental value is $36,000 per year. How is this arrangement treated for tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The rent-free use is a nontaxable fringe benefit because the shareholder manages the property", "$36,000 is a constructive dividend to the shareholder and is not deductible by Monroe Corp", "Monroe Corp can deduct $36,000 as a housing expense since it owns the condominium", "No tax consequences arise because no cash changed hands between the parties"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under constructive dividend principles, when a C corporation allows a shareholder to use corporate property rent-free, the FMV of that use ($36,000) is a constructive dividend to the extent of E&P. The corporation loses any deduction for the housing cost attributable to personal use. The shareholder reports $36,000 as dividend income. Wrong (A): Rent-free personal use of corporate property is not a nontaxable fringe benefit for a controlling shareholder. Wrong (C): The personal-use portion is not deductible as a business expense; it is a shareholder distribution. Wrong (D): Constructive dividends arise from economic benefits, not just cash transactions.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Complex Section 267 + Section 318 interaction
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A CPA advises the Grant family. Harold owns 35% of Vanguard Corp, his daughter owns 20%, and a trust benefiting Harold's grandchild owns 10%. Harold plans to sell rental property (basis $250,000, FMV $180,000) to Vanguard Corp for $180,000. The CPA must determine whether Section 267 disallows the $70,000 loss. Under Section 318, what is Harold's constructive ownership, and is the loss disallowed?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Harold constructively owns 55%; the loss is disallowed because he exceeds the 50% threshold", "Harold constructively owns 65%; the loss is disallowed because he exceeds the 50% threshold", "Harold constructively owns 45%; the loss is allowed because he does not exceed the 50% threshold", "Harold constructively owns 35%; only direct ownership counts for Section 267"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 318(a)(1), Harold is attributed his daughter's 20% (family: child) and his grandchild's trust interest of 10% (Section 318(a)(2)(B), beneficiary attribution). Harold's constructive ownership is 35% + 20% + 10% = 65%. Since Section 267(b)(2) disallows losses between an individual and a corporation when the individual owns more than 50% (directly or constructively), the $70,000 loss is disallowed. Wrong (A): 55% omits the 10% trust attribution. Wrong (C): 45% fails to include the trust attribution for the grandchild. Wrong (D): Section 267 explicitly incorporates constructive ownership under Section 318; direct ownership alone is insufficient.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — S corp debt basis restoration after profitable year
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$In Year 1, Patel's S corporation reports a $50,000 loss. Patel's stock basis is $30,000 and debt basis is $40,000. She deducts the full $50,000 loss (reducing stock to $0, debt to $20,000). In Year 2, the corporation reports $35,000 of ordinary income. After restoring basis, what are Patel's stock and debt basis amounts?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stock basis $35,000; debt basis $20,000", "Stock basis $0; debt basis $55,000", "Stock basis $20,000; debt basis $35,000", "Stock basis $15,000; debt basis $40,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1367 and Reg. 1.1367-2(c), when an S corporation has net income, debt basis that was previously reduced by losses is restored before stock basis is increased. Patel's debt was reduced by $20,000 (from $40,000 to $20,000). Year 2 net income of $35,000 first restores debt basis by $20,000 (debt returns to $40,000), then the remaining $15,000 increases stock basis from $0 to $15,000. Wrong (A): This fails to restore debt basis; income first restores previously reduced debt before increasing stock. Wrong (B): Income does not bypass stock and go entirely to debt; debt is restored first, then stock increases. Wrong (C): This does not correctly apply the restoration ordering; debt should be fully restored to $40,000 before stock increases.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Section 7872: demand vs term loan distinction
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A shareholder makes two below-market loans to her C corporation: a $300,000 demand loan at 1% interest (AFR is 5%) and a $200,000 term loan for 3 years at 0% interest (AFR is 4%). Under Section 7872, how is the foregone interest calculated differently for each loan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Demand loans use the AFR in effect during the year; term loans use present-value discounting at the AFR on the loan date", "Both loans use the same AFR calculation based on the loan origination date", "Demand loans are exempt from Section 7872; only term loans are subject to imputed interest", "Term loans use the AFR in effect during the year; demand loans use present-value discounting"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 7872, demand loans and term loans have different imputation methods. For demand loans, foregone interest is recalculated each year using the AFR in effect during that period (the difference between AFR interest and actual interest charged). For term loans, the foregone interest is determined at origination using present-value discounting—the excess of the loan amount over the present value of all payments discounted at the AFR. Wrong (B): The two loan types use fundamentally different calculation methods, not the same one. Wrong (C): Demand loans are fully subject to Section 7872; both types trigger imputed interest. Wrong (D): This reverses the correct methods for demand and term loans.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — What triggers Section 1239 recharacterization
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$What type of property triggers ordinary income recharacterization under Section 1239?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Any property sold between related parties", "Depreciable or amortizable property sold to a related party who will depreciate or amortize it", "Only real estate sold between related parties", "Capital assets held for more than one year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 1239 recharacterizes gain as ordinary income when depreciable or amortizable property is sold between related parties (as defined in Section 1239(b), including a person and a more-than-50%-owned entity). The purpose is to prevent the seller from claiming capital gain while the buyer takes depreciation deductions on the stepped-up basis. Wrong (A): Section 1239 applies only to depreciable/amortizable property, not all property types. Wrong (C): Section 1239 covers all depreciable property, not just real estate—equipment, patents, and other amortizable assets also qualify. Wrong (D): Capital assets are not the trigger; the property must be depreciable or amortizable in the hands of the buyer.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Section 302(b)(1) meaningful reduction test
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Thornton is a minority shareholder owning 25% of a publicly traded corporation. The corporation redeems a portion of Thornton's shares, reducing his ownership to 22%. The redemption does not meet the substantially disproportionate test under Section 302(b)(2). Under Section 302(b)(1), what standard applies to determine if the redemption qualifies for sale/exchange treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The redemption must reduce ownership by at least 20 percentage points", "The redemption must reduce ownership below 10% of outstanding shares", "The redemption must result in a meaningful reduction in the shareholder's proportionate interest based on facts and circumstances", "The redemption automatically fails if the 302(b)(2) test is not met"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 302(b)(1), a redemption qualifies for sale/exchange treatment if it is not essentially equivalent to a dividend, determined by whether there is a meaningful reduction in the shareholder's proportionate interest. This is a facts-and-circumstances test established in United States v. Davis (1970). For minority shareholders with no control, even a small reduction may qualify. Wrong (A): There is no fixed 20-percentage-point threshold under Section 302(b)(1). Wrong (B): No 10% threshold exists under this test. Wrong (D): Section 302(b)(1) is an independent test; failing 302(b)(2) does not preclude 302(b)(1) qualification.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L2 — Constructive dividend: loans with no repayment intent
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Ryland Corp, a C corporation, advances $150,000 to its sole shareholder. No promissory note is executed, no interest is charged, no repayment date is set, and no repayments have been made in three years. The corporation has sufficient E&P. How does the IRS likely treat this advance?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As a tax-free loan because no formal dividend was declared", "As deductible compensation to the shareholder", "As a nontaxable return of capital", "As a constructive dividend of $150,000 to the shareholder"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): When a corporation makes advances to a shareholder with no note, no interest, no repayment schedule, and no actual repayments, the IRS treats the advance as a constructive dividend rather than a loan. The lack of genuine debt characteristics indicates there was no intent to repay. The $150,000 is dividend income to the extent of E&P. Wrong (A): The absence of a formal declaration does not prevent the IRS from recharacterizing the transaction as a dividend. Wrong (B): Compensation treatment requires the shareholder to be performing services; the advance itself is not compensation. Wrong (C): Return of capital treatment applies to actual distributions exceeding E&P, not to disguised loans.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Owner-to-entity attribution under Section 318
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 318(a)(3), what is the owner-to-entity attribution rule?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stock owned by a partner or 50%-or-more shareholder is attributed to the partnership or corporation", "Stock owned by a corporation is attributed to all shareholders regardless of ownership percentage", "Stock owned by an entity is attributed only to family members of the entity's owners", "Stock is attributed from entities to owners, not from owners to entities"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 318(a)(3), stock owned by a partner is attributed to the partnership (regardless of partnership interest size), and stock owned by a shareholder who owns 50% or more of a corporation is attributed to that corporation. This is the owner-to-entity direction of attribution. Wrong (B): This describes entity-to-owner attribution under Section 318(a)(2), not owner-to-entity, and it only applies to 50%+ shareholders for corporations. Wrong (C): Owner-to-entity attribution attributes the owner's stock to the entity itself, not to family members. Wrong (D): Section 318 provides both directions of attribution: entity-to-owner under 318(a)(2) and owner-to-entity under 318(a)(3).$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Section 318 option attribution
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Nichols owns 40% of Crestview Corp's outstanding stock. Nichols also holds unexercised options to purchase an additional 15% of Crestview's stock. For purposes of Section 318 constructive ownership, what is Nichols's total ownership percentage?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["40%, because unexercised options are not counted until exercised", "55%, because Section 318 treats option holders as owning the underlying stock", "40%, because option attribution only applies to publicly traded corporations", "55%, but only if the options are exercisable within the current tax year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 318(a)(4), a person holding an option to acquire stock is treated as owning that stock for constructive ownership purposes. Nichols's 40% direct ownership plus 15% option attribution equals 55% total constructive ownership. This applies regardless of whether the options have been exercised. Wrong (A): Section 318(a)(4) treats unexercised options as ownership; exercise is not required. Wrong (C): Option attribution applies to all corporations, not just publicly traded ones. Wrong (D): There is no current-year exercisability requirement; all outstanding options trigger attribution.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2)
;

